# CHANGELOG

## 20260913

0.4.0: + the other way a tag and a remote disagree: origin not having it at all.

1. + bin/release-audit: remote_tag_findings() reports a tag which is here and not on origin. It had asked only whether the tags origin holds name the commits ours do, and skipped a tag origin lacked with `next if remote.nil?`, so the one case a push does not correct on its own went unreported. --follow-tags carries only tags reachable from the commits being pushed, so a tag cut onto old history stays here: eventful had seven after a backfill and the check said nothing.
2. - bin/release-audit: REMOTE_TAGS, which asked about the newest five versions only. The cost of the check is the single ls-remote call, which answers with every tag at once, so comparing five or five hundred costs the same; the window was guarding against output volume, which listed() already handles. Dropping it found two divergences it had been hiding, stateful's v2.3.3 and git-import-all's v0.14.1, and stateful 2.3.3 is published.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: the usage text, which described one of the two questions --origin now asks.
4. ~ README.md: + the Options table and the two origin findings. "What it does not report yet" named the remote tag check, which --origin has done since 0.3.0.
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.3.0/0.4.0/

0.3.0: + --origin, which asks whether the tags origin holds name the commits ours do.

1. + bin/release-audit: remote_tag_findings(), read_remote_refs() and remote_tag_commit(). A push moves commits and leaves tags where they are, so a rewritten history leaves the remote naming commits it has abandoned while every local check reads clean. Behind a switch because it is the only thing here which touches the network.
2. The peeled `^{}` line is preferred where there is one: an annotated tag names a tag object and the commit beneath it is what a checkout gets, so comparing the tag object against a commit reports every annotated tag as diverged.
3. Only the newest REMOTE_TAGS versions are asked about. A repository with 77 tags would otherwise report on all of them, and a tag which has stood for years is not what a rewrite abandons.
4. + bin/release-audit: prefetch_remote_refs(), which makes the calls together rather than one after another. Sequentially the run took 5m17s against 12.9s for the whole of the rest of it; sixteen threads bring it to 43.7s, with the findings identical. Each call is spent waiting, and Ruby gives up the lock for the duration of a subprocess.
5. Having no tags is nothing to compare rather than a remote which declined to answer, and ls-remote says nothing in both cases. Asked the other way round first, thirty repositories reported a remote which had answered perfectly well.
6. Run over the estate it found four stale tags across three repositories: duration.rb v0.8.0, git-import-all v0.14.2 and v0.14.3, and imap v0.4.1. None is visible from a clone.
7. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.2.1/0.3.0/, and the date.

## 20260911

0.2.1: ~ the count: three standings, so that work in progress is not counted as a release left half done.

1. + bin/release-audit: standing(), and WORKING beside CURRENT and OLDER. A release which stopped between being made and being delivered is CURRENT — a latest version with no tag, or commits the remote has not seen. A tree with changes in it is WORKING: it is work rather than a release, though it makes every later column provisional and so is still said. Everything else is OLDER.
2. Counted as one, the line read `48 of 86 repositories have a release left half done`, and 0.2.0's split of the findings left it contradicting the rows beneath it. Of those 48, 24 were there only for a dirty tree and not one was a latest version untagged. It now reads 13, 24 and 11, and the 13 is a number which can be acted upon.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: summary() builds its clauses rather than stating one sentence, there being three numbers where there was one and any of them possibly zero.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: a root holding no repositories of ours said `Nothing outstanding in 0 repositories`, which reads as reassurance where nothing was examined. It now says so.
5. ~ bin/release-audit: the constants are in alphabetical order after the defaults.
6. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.2.0/0.2.1/

0.2.0: Separate a repository which has never been tagged from one whose latest release is untagged.

1. + bin/release-audit: untagged_findings(), which distinguishes three states where there was one. A repository with no tags at all says `never tagged, 21 versions claimed` and says it once; a latest claimed version with no tag says so by name, which is the only one of the three that is a release left half done; and versions behind a tagged latest say `4 earlier untagged`, which is backfill and is done years after the fact here by design.
2. The one finding it replaces reported all three identically, so dsv's 77 and shellac's 21 read as urgently as a release which had genuinely stopped half way. Run over the estate, the urgent case fires for no repository at all: every latest is tagged, and every tagging finding was historical.
3. - bin/release-audit: untagged_versions(), which nothing calls now.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.1.2/0.2.0/

0.1.2: ~ the name column: fitted to the longest name reported rather than fixed at 24.

1. + bin/release-audit: name_width() and row(), the width taken from the names actually reported and floored at MINIMUM_NAME_WIDTH so that a short run is not cramped. ROW is gone, a constant being the wrong shape for something computed per run.
2. A name wider than the column pushed every column after it out of true, which the headings added at 0.1.1 made obvious and which nothing showed before: whitehorse-gutter-cleaning is 26 characters. Truncating was the alternative and would have cost the one column which identifies the row.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.1.1/0.1.2/

0.1.1: + column headings, the repository column headed only where it is asked for.

1. + bin/release-audit: headings(), and ROW, which the headings and the rows now share so that they cannot drift apart. A run over the estate reports 48 repositories, and reading a column of bare values with nothing saying what they are is what made that overwhelming rather than informative.
2. + bin/release-audit: -n, --name, which heads the repository column. It is off by default: a column of repository names says what it is, and the heading is one more line between the reader and the report.
3. ~ bin/release-audit: - the `<--` before each row's findings, the column now being headed `outstanding`.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: ROW joins the other constants at the top rather than sitting above report().
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.1.0/0.1.1/

0.1.0: Audit only the repositories which are ours, and say less about the ones which have never been tagged.

1. ~ bin/release-audit: + ours?(), which takes the root commit author as the proxy section 4 of missing-tools-note.md settles on. A fork cannot be released from here, so every column is meaningless for one: the first run reported homebrew-core with 249 uncommitted files, and rails, sorbet, crystal, direnv and two dotfiles repositories beside it. 171 repositories fall to 86 and 61 findings to 48.
2. ~ bin/release-audit: + copy?(), which now reads .bak, .old and .orig as well as the Finder's " copy". changelogger.bak and imap.bak were each reported as a repository of their own.
3. + bin/release-audit: listed(), naming the first six untagged versions and counting the rest. dsv has never been tagged and claims 77 versions, which filled a line and told nobody anything.
4. ~ bin/release-audit: + committed(), so that one file does not read as `1 files`.
5. ~ bin/release-audit: /0.0.0/0.1.0/

0.0.0: Report which repositories beneath a root have a release left half done.

1. + bin/release-audit: a walk for .git directories beneath a root, and per repository three columns which need no network — whether the tree is committed, which versions it claims and has tagged, and whether the branch is pushed. Section 4 of missing-tools-note.md describes five; the two which ask the outside world, whether the tag on the remote is the tag here and whether the version is published, are not asked yet.
2. + bin/release-audit: the versions a repository claims are taken from its commit subjects, in the form `1.2.3: summary`. The changelog says it too, in three different filenames and several formats, so it is left for later.
3. + bin/release-audit: the root comes from --root, else the configured one, else the current directory, as tap-audit takes its two directories.
4. + README.md, LICENSE, .gitignore, CHANGELOG.
